%[text] # Fluid Statics
%[text] An aquarium tank has a window AB for viewing the inhabitants.  The width of the window is fixed at `w = 1.5`  m.  The top of the window is shown as 2 m below the surface, but should be parameterized as the variable `a` .The height of the window is shown as 3 m, but should be considered variable `b`  .   The tank contains water with a density of 1000 kg/m^3.
%[text] ![](text:image:7ae2)
%[text] Input variables: depth of the top of the window (a), height of the window (b) and width of the window (w) - all in meters.
a = 2;
b = 3;
w = 1.5;
%[text] Begin by drawing a diagram of the window showing the load intensity and the equivalent concentrated force.
%[text] ![](text:image:9ed3)
%[text] The pressure at the top and the bottom of the window are
%[text] $P\_A = \\rho g a$
%[text] $P\_B = \\rho g (a+b)$
rho = 1000;
g = 9.81;
Pa = rho*g*a %[output:5e30247b]
Pb = rho*g*(a+b) %[output:75926d10]
%[text] Since the loading is linear, the average pressure acting on the window is
Pave = (Pa + Pb)/2 %[output:46d188db]
%[text] The tital force acting on the window is the average pressure times the area of the window
F = Pave*(b*w) %[output:23f851f3]
%[text] Expressed in kN, and rounded to the nearest integer, the average force is
round(F/1e3) %[output:5305976f]

